WEBVTT

00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:07.478
Welcome to CSC4801, AI-assisted Software Engineering.

00:00:07.478 --> 00:00:11.020
This is Lecture 5: How to Write a Prompt.

00:00:11.800 --> 00:00:21.840
Here is a map of the parts around a coding agent: context management, skills, RAG, MCP, a sandbox, and subagents.

00:00:22.190 --> 00:00:26.990
Today we zoom in on the place where everything starts: the prompt.

00:00:27.340 --> 00:00:33.083
The title of this part plays on a famous paper, Attention Is All You Need.

00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:37.740
Ours is: Prompt Is All You Need, by all students in CSC4801.

00:00:38.090 --> 00:00:41.478
Because every one of you writes prompts already.

00:00:41.478 --> 00:00:47.690
The real questions are how to write them well, and how to tell when the result is right.

00:00:48.467 --> 00:00:51.852
Writing a prompt has two key parts.

00:00:51.852 --> 00:00:53.787
Specify, and verify.

00:00:54.137 --> 00:00:57.962
To specify, you state the goal and what counts as done,

00:00:57.962 --> 00:01:01.857
and you supply the context and examples the model needs.

00:01:02.207 --> 00:01:05.619
To verify, you set up the workflow and the tools,

00:01:05.619 --> 00:01:07.847
then you run tests, and iterate.

00:01:08.197 --> 00:01:10.002
Look at the whole path.

00:01:10.002 --> 00:01:13.220
Your intent becomes a task specification.

00:01:13.220 --> 00:01:15.652
The model produces a candidate.

00:01:15.652 --> 00:01:18.164
Then comes review and execution.

00:01:18.164 --> 00:01:22.637
You observe how it behaves, and you accept it, or revise.

00:01:22.987 --> 00:01:26.526
A better prompt gives you better candidates.

00:01:26.526 --> 00:01:30.627
But accepting one still needs engineering evidence.

00:01:31.227 --> 00:01:35.941
So before you write a long prompt, keep three questions visible.

00:01:35.941 --> 00:01:40.507
What should change? What must stay true? And how will we know?

00:01:40.857 --> 00:01:42.516
Why does this matter?

00:01:42.516 --> 00:01:45.676
Because a prompt is a conditional input.

00:01:45.676 --> 00:01:49.784
Instructions describe what to do and how to respond.

00:01:49.784 --> 00:01:53.497
Context supplies facts, examples, and evidence.

00:01:53.847 --> 00:01:59.468
The model responds to the context it has, not to the intent in your head.

00:01:59.468 --> 00:02:03.087
Anything you don't write down, it has to guess.

00:02:03.867 --> 00:02:07.107
Here is a starting point: six boxes to fill in.

00:02:07.457 --> 00:02:09.692
Goal: what should change?

00:02:09.692 --> 00:02:12.196
Context: which facts matter?

00:02:12.196 --> 00:02:15.057
Input: what should be processed?

00:02:15.407 --> 00:02:18.366
Constraints: what must remain true?

00:02:18.366 --> 00:02:21.155
Output: what should be delivered?

00:02:21.155 --> 00:02:23.607
And checks: how will we know?

00:02:23.957 --> 00:02:26.286
The three questions are all in there.

00:02:26.286 --> 00:02:30.629
This is a classroom checklist, not an official framework with a name.

00:02:30.629 --> 00:02:32.517
Use it to spot what's missing.

00:02:32.867 --> 00:02:37.041
Start with the goal, and give it a clear verb and object.

00:02:37.041 --> 00:02:42.827
Help with my Python code, or, make it better, leaves every choice to the model.

00:02:43.177 --> 00:02:49.475
Compare that with: fix empty-list handling in the average function of stats.py.

00:02:49.475 --> 00:02:52.983
The verb is fix. The object is one function.

00:02:52.983 --> 00:02:54.817
Now the scope is clear.

00:02:55.167 --> 00:03:01.327
A clear scope means less accidental work, and fewer hidden choices.

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:05.489
Next, define the behavior you can observe.

00:03:05.489 --> 00:03:07.183
Here is our function.

00:03:07.183 --> 00:03:11.540
It returns the sum of the list, divided by its length.

00:03:11.890 --> 00:03:14.049
Pause and think for a moment.

00:03:14.049 --> 00:03:16.730
What happens when the list is empty?

00:03:17.280 --> 00:03:21.559
The length is zero, so Python raises a division by zero error.

00:03:21.559 --> 00:03:23.560
That's the bug we want fixed.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:25.623
Now write the contract.

00:03:25.623 --> 00:03:30.613
The input is a list of finite numbers, and empty lists are allowed.

00:03:30.613 --> 00:03:33.816
For the output, an empty list returns None.

00:03:33.816 --> 00:03:36.870
Otherwise, the function returns the mean.

00:03:37.220 --> 00:03:40.300
An edge case is part of the requirement.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.380
It is not a matter of the model's taste.

00:03:43.980 --> 00:03:47.519
Put it all together, and you get a complete request.

00:03:47.519 --> 00:03:50.310
Fix the average function for empty lists.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:53.100
State the input, and the expected output.

00:03:53.450 --> 00:03:54.842
Then the constraints.

00:03:54.842 --> 00:04:00.410
Preserve the signature and the input list, and use only the Python standard library.

00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.956
Ask for something you can check: a minimal diff, plus test evidence.

00:04:05.956 --> 00:04:07.178
Attach stats.py.

00:04:07.178 --> 00:04:09.394
Include those three examples.

00:04:09.394 --> 00:04:13.520
And ask the model to tell you if the tests cannot run.

00:04:13.870 --> 00:04:20.470
It's short, it's concrete, and every line is tied to an outcome you can check.

00:04:21.267 --> 00:04:24.098
A popular trick is the role prompt.

00:04:24.098 --> 00:04:28.467
You are a senior engineer. Review this code carefully.

00:04:28.817 --> 00:04:32.220
A role can guide the style and the point of view.

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:34.442
But it does not grant expertise.

00:04:34.442 --> 00:04:38.817
The model doesn't become a senior engineer because you said so.

00:04:39.167 --> 00:04:41.304
What helps more is a method.

00:04:41.304 --> 00:04:43.364
Check the input boundaries.

00:04:43.364 --> 00:04:45.349
Find one concrete failure.

00:04:45.349 --> 00:04:47.104
Cite the relevant line.

00:04:47.104 --> 00:04:50.767
Now the model knows what a careful review means.

00:04:51.117 --> 00:04:52.482
Real systems do both.

00:04:52.482 --> 00:04:59.437
Here is one of the system prompts inside Claude Code, for an agent that reviews the rules of its auto mode.

00:04:59.787 --> 00:05:02.112
It starts by assigning a role.

00:05:02.112 --> 00:05:05.833
Then it explains the setting and states the job.

00:05:05.833 --> 00:05:13.507
And then it lists the exact criteria to check: clarity, completeness, conflicts, and actionability.

00:05:13.857 --> 00:05:18.637
It even says what to do when all the rules look good.

00:05:18.637 --> 00:05:20.802
The role sets the scene.

00:05:20.802 --> 00:05:23.057
The method does the work.

00:05:23.834 --> 00:05:26.940
Next, keep instructions and data apart.

00:05:26.940 --> 00:05:32.434
The task says: summarize the incident, and use only the supplied log.

00:05:32.784 --> 00:05:36.788
The log itself goes inside tags, here called incident log.

00:05:36.788 --> 00:05:42.104
Now the model can tell your instructions from the material it should work on.

00:05:42.454 --> 00:05:48.534
And if the log happens to contain a sentence that looks like an instruction, it stays data.

00:05:48.884 --> 00:05:52.868
When the task is about code, give a minimal code region.

00:05:52.868 --> 00:06:00.764
Where: the target path and function, the relevant caller or interface, and the runtime and dependency versions.

00:06:01.114 --> 00:06:08.274
Why, and how to check: the expected behavior, a minimal failing example, and the right test command.

00:06:08.624 --> 00:06:13.559
Give enough context to tell the intended patch apart from a guess.

00:06:13.559 --> 00:06:18.344
Not the whole repository. Just the part that decides the answer.

00:06:19.134 --> 00:06:20.170
Now, examples.

00:06:20.170 --> 00:06:23.428
Zero-shot prompting means instructions only.

00:06:23.428 --> 00:06:29.054
Classify the issue as a bug, a feature, or a question, and return one label.

00:06:29.404 --> 00:06:31.665
How do I export CSV files?

00:06:31.665 --> 00:06:33.231
That's a question.

00:06:33.231 --> 00:06:38.884
Try a clear instruction like this first, before you add examples.

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:40.778
Here's a harder one.

00:06:40.778 --> 00:06:45.331
Extract the drink order from this message, and output JSON.

00:06:45.331 --> 00:06:50.194
A large iced latte, less sugar, with an extra shot of espresso.

00:06:50.544 --> 00:06:54.148
What field names will you get back? Size, or cup?

00:06:54.148 --> 00:06:56.281
Sugar as a word, or a number?

00:06:56.281 --> 00:07:01.944
The model has to invent a format, and it may change from one run to the next.

00:07:02.494 --> 00:07:05.528
Few-shot prompting adds examples.

00:07:05.528 --> 00:07:08.101
Crashes on startup is a bug.

00:07:08.101 --> 00:07:10.675
Add CSV export is a feature.

00:07:10.675 --> 00:07:13.525
Where is export, is a question.

00:07:13.525 --> 00:07:17.294
So, CSV export is missing rows, is a bug.

00:07:17.644 --> 00:07:25.455
For the drink order, two worked examples show the exact fields: size, drink, temperature, sugar, and extras.

00:07:25.455 --> 00:07:28.204
Now the model fills in the same shape.

00:07:28.554 --> 00:07:32.914
Examples can show a decision boundary, or a local convention.

00:07:33.264 --> 00:07:35.480
So choose them for coverage.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:40.704
Three obvious bug reports, worded the same way, teach very little.

00:07:41.054 --> 00:07:48.170
A stronger set has one example per label, a case that looks ambiguous, and a consistent output format.

00:07:48.170 --> 00:07:53.054
What matters is coverage of your task, not a magic number of examples.

00:07:53.834 --> 00:07:58.594
You have met chain of thought before: let's think step by step.

00:07:58.944 --> 00:08:00.162
But be careful.

00:08:00.162 --> 00:08:06.738
The results depend on the model and the task setting, and more text is not proof.

00:08:06.738 --> 00:08:12.664
Treat published prompting results as evidence to test, not as guarantees.

00:08:13.264 --> 00:08:16.024
A planning prompt has the same trap.

00:08:16.024 --> 00:08:19.244
Understand, implement, test, is too vague.

00:08:19.244 --> 00:08:21.544
Each step could mean anything.

00:08:21.894 --> 00:08:26.423
A plan with decisions says: read the empty-input contract.

00:08:26.423 --> 00:08:28.688
Add only an empty-list guard.

00:08:28.688 --> 00:08:31.734
Run the edge-case and regression tests.

00:08:32.084 --> 00:08:35.604
A good plan names what each step will resolve.

00:08:36.204 --> 00:08:38.794
One more technique: structured output.

00:08:38.794 --> 00:08:47.724
Ask the model to review a function and return JSON only, with fields for the bug, the severity, the reason, and the corrected code.

00:08:48.074 --> 00:08:50.947
Now a program can read the answer directly.

00:08:50.947 --> 00:08:54.354
Structured output makes the result easy to process.

00:08:55.134 --> 00:08:58.585
Here is a template that collects it all.

00:08:58.585 --> 00:09:03.934
Goal, context and input, constraints, deliverable, and checks.

00:09:04.284 --> 00:09:11.364
Plus one line people often forget: if you are blocked, report the missing fact instead of guessing.

00:09:11.714 --> 00:09:21.354
You can also add a relevant example, a short staged workflow, a source or a version, or a budget and a stopping rule.

00:09:21.704 --> 00:09:24.792
But adapt it, don't copy it blindly.

00:09:24.792 --> 00:09:29.424
Delete any field that adds no value to your real task.

00:09:30.024 --> 00:09:33.224
The habit worth keeping has four parts.

00:09:33.224 --> 00:09:35.357
Make your intent explicit.

00:09:35.357 --> 00:09:37.408
Bring the right evidence.

00:09:37.408 --> 00:09:38.967
Check the artifact.

00:09:38.967 --> 00:09:41.264
And revise from the failure.

00:09:41.614 --> 00:09:45.202
A good prompt starts a reliable handoff.

00:09:45.202 --> 00:09:47.534
Verification completes it.

00:09:48.334 --> 00:09:53.151
But what if the output is still wrong after several attempts?

00:09:53.151 --> 00:09:54.414
Is it game over?

00:09:54.964 --> 00:09:55.660
Not yet.

00:09:55.660 --> 00:09:59.924
There is another lever, called test-time scaling.

00:10:00.274 --> 00:10:06.461
Training-time compute uses training data and optimization to update the model's weights.

00:10:06.461 --> 00:10:09.554
That cost is shared across all future tasks.

00:10:09.904 --> 00:10:12.997
Compute at test time keeps the weights fixed.

00:10:12.997 --> 00:10:16.984
Instead, it spends extra inference effort on one new task.

00:10:17.334 --> 00:10:24.534
So the same model can try harder on a hard problem, without any retraining.

00:10:25.301 --> 00:10:28.627
There are three ways to spend that effort.

00:10:28.627 --> 00:10:31.636
Sequential: one path, with more steps.

00:10:31.636 --> 00:10:34.170
Parallel: more paths to compare.

00:10:34.170 --> 00:10:37.021
And search: choose where to explore.

00:10:37.371 --> 00:10:42.483
Sequential scaling means reasoning longer, or revising after feedback.

00:10:42.483 --> 00:10:47.011
Say the first draft makes every customer pay 80% of the price.

00:10:47.361 --> 00:10:52.043
A test gives feedback: for a nonmember, we expected 100, but got 80.

00:10:52.043 --> 00:10:57.001
The revision gives the discount only to members, and then we test again.

00:10:57.351 --> 00:11:03.192
The second test must run on the revised code, not reuse an old result.

00:11:03.192 --> 00:11:07.031
Extra steps help when they bring new evidence.

00:11:07.631 --> 00:11:12.229
Parallel scaling samples several candidates, then selects one.

00:11:12.229 --> 00:11:15.047
Self-consistency votes on the answers.

00:11:15.047 --> 00:11:17.791
Best of N ranks them with a verifier.

00:11:18.141 --> 00:11:20.149
Here's why it is attractive.

00:11:20.149 --> 00:11:25.815
Say one sample is right a quarter of the time, and the samples are independent.

00:11:25.815 --> 00:11:30.621
With 8 samples, the chance that at least one is right is about 90%.

00:11:30.971 --> 00:11:35.761
But a correct candidate only helps if the selector can find it.

00:11:35.761 --> 00:11:39.411
Both methods depend on the quality of selection.

00:11:40.201 --> 00:11:42.424
Search goes one step further.

00:11:42.424 --> 00:11:46.641
Start from a bug report, and generate a few hypotheses.

00:11:46.991 --> 00:11:48.302
Evaluate each branch.

00:11:48.302 --> 00:11:54.231
Expand the promising ones, prune or backtrack from the rest, and stop when the budget runs out.

00:11:54.581 --> 00:12:01.421
Search spends compute on promising intermediate steps, not just on complete answers.

00:12:02.021 --> 00:12:04.784
So how much effort should a task get?

00:12:04.784 --> 00:12:10.461
If it's clear and easy: one attempt, a cheap check, and stop when it passes.

00:12:10.811 --> 00:12:19.811
If it's hard but checkable: try diverse candidates, use informative feedback, and keep some budget for verification.

00:12:20.161 --> 00:12:24.239
And if information is missing, more retries won't help.

00:12:24.239 --> 00:12:26.242
Retrieve the facts, or ask.

00:12:26.242 --> 00:12:29.801
Change the approach, and stop the blind retries.

00:12:30.151 --> 00:12:32.482
And if it still doesn't work?

00:12:32.482 --> 00:12:35.455
The deck jokes that you may need AGI.

00:12:35.455 --> 00:12:38.831
Until then, go back to specify and verify.

00:12:39.601 --> 00:12:40.881
Let's sum up.

00:12:41.231 --> 00:12:44.699
A prompt has two parts: specify, and verify.

00:12:44.699 --> 00:12:51.951
Keep three questions visible: what should change, what must stay true, and how will we know.

00:12:52.301 --> 00:13:00.421
Give a clear verb and object, a contract with its edge cases, just the right context, and examples chosen for coverage.

00:13:00.771 --> 00:13:08.735
If the output is still wrong, spend test-time effort wisely: revise with feedback, sample and select, or search.

00:13:08.735 --> 00:13:11.011
And when facts are missing, ask.

00:13:11.361 --> 00:13:13.016
Here's something to try.

00:13:13.016 --> 00:13:17.222
Take a prompt you wrote this week, and fill in the six boxes.

00:13:17.222 --> 00:13:18.601
Which box was empty?

00:13:19.401 --> 00:13:20.894
That's all for this lecture.

00:13:20.894 --> 00:13:23.721
Bring your questions to the next class, Requirements.

